# Git writes on explicit order

Extends BASE §3 *Git writes*. Local git **read** commands may run without asking. Remote writes cover `git push` in any form (`git -C … push`) and any other remote-write subcommand; "publish the branch" also counts as a push order. Headless factory workers (`AGENTCTL_FACTORY`) follow `.agentctl [git.factory]` instead: the factory may commit and push the task branch (merging the base with `--no-ff`, never a fast-forward, never a force push or a protected branch), then merge the task branch with `--no-ff` into the review branch its repo's `.agentctl [review_env]` table names and push that branch, because the review environment deploys from it (`[git.factory] deploy_review`; never a protected branch, never resolving a conflict there), and every other ask becomes a deny. MR, PR, review request: see BASE §3 *Review requests*.

- Index: stage or unstage only when the user asked to stage, unstage, or commit, and only the paths of that request. Otherwise leave agent edits **unstaged**: staged is the user's baseline, unstaged is new agent work.
- Reset: `git reset` in any form (to unstage, move `HEAD`, or undo a commit) runs only on an explicit reset order. Never use it to "clean up" a user's commit or to approximate an amend.
- Amend on explicit order (date-only, message fix, hook-touched files): run it even if `HEAD` was not created by the agent in this chat; this overrides the default gate. Date-only: `agentctl git amend-date <ISO-8601 datetime with offset>`; it refuses a pushed `HEAD` unless `--allow-pushed`. BASE remote-amend (warn if already pushed) and hook rules still apply.
- Commit messages hold only the intended title and body: no `Co-authored-by:` in any casing, no "Generated with …" line, no trailer attributing the commit to another author or tool. One exception, by operator order: every commit and review request (MR/PR) description the `agentctl factory` creates ends with a blank line and the signature in `.agentctl [git.factory] signature` (`Automated by: agentctl factory`). Nothing else gets it.
- Task worktrees stay checked out on their task branch, never detached `HEAD` (including `git worktree add --detach`) unless the user asks.
- Merges never fast-forward, even on linear history: always `git merge --no-ff`. Never `--ff`, `--ff-only`, a merge that fast-forwards because `merge.ff` is unset, or `git pull --ff-only` onto the target branch. Finishing a feature-branch commit is not permission to fast-forward the integration branch.
